===Etymology===
An {{apocopic form|enm|min|nocap=1}}, {{m-lite|enm|myn}}, from {{inh-lite|enm|ang|min|mīn|my, mine}}, from {{inh-lite|enm|gem-pro|sc=Latnx|*mīnaz||pos=pron.|my, mine}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA-lite|enm|/miː/}}
* {{a|unstressed}} {{IPA-lite|enm|/mi/}}

===Determiner===
{{head-lite|enm|determiners|nominative|I}}

# {{n-g-lite|First-person singular genitive determiner:}} [[my]].
#* {{RQ:Wycliffe NT Lichfield|John|2|16|45|r|column=2|And he ſeide to hem þat ſelden culueris / take ȝe awei from hennes þeſe þingis .· ⁊ nyle ȝe make þe hous of '''my''' fadir an hows of marchaundiſe|And he said to those who sold doves: "Take those things out of here; you won't make '''my''' father's house a place of business!"}}

====Usage notes====
''mi'' is usually used before a consonant (other than ''h-''), while ''{{l-lite|enm|min}}'' is usually used before a vowel or ''h-'', much as with Modern English ''[[an#English|an]]'' vs ''[[a#English|a]]''.

