==Welsh==

===Etymology===
From {{inh-lite|cy|cel-bry-pro|sc=Latnx|*mi}}, from {{inh-lite|cy|cel-pro|sc=Latnx|*mī}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA-lite|cy|/miː/}}
* {{rhymes-lite|cy|iː|s=1}}

===Pronoun===
{{head-lite|cy|pronouns|cat2=personal pronouns}}

# [[I]], [[me]]
#: {{ux-lite|cy|Mae hen wlad fy nhadau yn annwyl i '''mi'''.|The old land of my fathers is dear to '''me'''.}}
#: {{ux-lite|cy|Rhaid i '''mi''' fynd i weld Taid.|'''I''' have to go and see Granddad.}}

====Usage notes====
''Mi'' is typically heard only after the preposition {{m-lite|cy|i||to, for}} in formal language and in northern colloquial language. In southern colloquial language the form {{m-lite|cy|fi}} is used after the preposition {{m-lite|cy|i}}.

====See also====
* {{l-lite|cy|fi||I, me}}
* {{l-lite|cy|i||I, me}}

===Particle===
{{head-lite|cy|particles|triggers [[soft mutation]] on the following verb}}

# {{lb|cy|North Wales}} {{n-g-lite|used with inflected verbs to mark affirmative statements.}}
#: {{ux-lite|cy|'''Mi''' werthes i hanner dwsin.|I sold half a dozen.}}

====Usage notes====
* This particle is optional and may only be used before inflected verbs in the preterite, future or conditional in affirmative statements, e.g. {{m-lite|cy||mi}} {{m-lite|cy|fydda}} {{m-lite|cy|i}}{{m-lite|cy|'n}} {{m-lite|cy|mynd||I will go}}.
* Some speakers may drop the particle but keep the resulting soft mutation, e.g. {{m-lite|cy|fydda}} {{m-lite|cy|i}}{{m-lite|cy|'n}} {{m-lite|cy|mynd||I will go}} instead of {{m-lite|cy|bydda}} {{m-lite|cy|i}}{{m-lite|cy|'n}} {{m-lite|cy|mynd}}.

====Synonyms====
* {{l-lite|cy|fe}} {{q-lite|South Wales}}

